  {
    tier: 'Senior correspondent, bureau chief, editor',
    detail: 'Pay rises meaningfully at this level, but openings are scarce and tied directly to how well the specific outlet is doing financially — a senior title at a struggling outlet does not guarantee senior pay.',
  },
];

const crisisItems = [
  {
    label: 'Revenue pressure',
    title: 'Print advertising has been shrinking for years',
    body: 'Digital ad spend keeps pulling budgets away from print, and most Indian newspapers still depend heavily on print advertising to fund their newsrooms. When that revenue shrinks, the newsroom budget shrinks with it — reporters, not executives, usually feel that first.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Consolidation',
    title: 'Outlets are merging, cutting desks, or shutting down entirely',
    body: 'Indian media layoffs rose roughly 15% year-on-year through 2025, and global media and entertainment companies cut more than 17,000 jobs in 2025 alone, a trend that has continued into 2026 as more outlets restructure around leaner, digital-first teams.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Ownership pressure',
    title: 'Editorial independence and financial survival do not always point the same way',
    body: 'Some outlets that depend on advertisers, government access, or a single owner face real pressure on what they can report freely. This affects the day-to-day work, not just the paycheck — reporters sometimes have to navigate what a story costs the outlet politically, not only whether it is true.',
  },
];

const pressFreedomItems = [
  {
    label: 'Documented pattern',
    title: 'India consistently ranks in the lower half of global press-freedom indexes',
    body: 'Reporters Without Borders has placed India in the bottom third of its World Press Freedom Index in recent years, citing harassment, legal pressure, and violence against journalists as recurring, not isolated, problems.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Documented pattern',
    title: 'Field and investigative reporters carry the highest real risk',
    body: 'Journalists covering land disputes, mining, communal tension, organised crime, or state and corporate power have faced physical attacks, intimidation, sedition or UAPA cases, and in the most serious documented cases, killed for their reporting. This risk is concentrated in specific beats, not spread evenly across every journalism job.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Documented pattern',
    title: 'Online harassment hits women journalists disproportionately',
    body: 'Coordinated harassment campaigns, doxxing, and threats are a documented, ongoing problem for women reporters covering politics, religion, and gender, often with little institutional protection from the employer.',
  },
];

const digitalGrid = [
  {
    label: 'Traditional, employed journalism',
    title: 'Structured pay, shrinking pool',
    body: 'A fixed monthly salary and an editor to answer to, with a handful of statutory protections for staff reporters at established outlets — but the number of these roles falls a little further every year as print and TV budgets tighten.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Digital and independent journalism',
    title: 'Wide open, financially unstable',
    body: 'Subscriber-funded outlets (Newslaundry), crowdfunded newsrooms (The Print, The Wire), and independent creator-journalists on YouTube and newsletters have real reach and real editorial freedom — but income depends on subscribers, sponsors, or ad revenue that can swing month to month, with no salary floor.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Fit',
    body: 'Journalism rewards people who get real energy from chasing a moving story, confronting uncomfortable facts, and publishing on someone else\'s clock and someone else\'s scrutiny. It does not reward people who simply enjoy writing — that instinct is better served by content writing, which has a steadier process and a much lower financial and physical risk profile.',
    note: 'If the idea of a source going cold an hour before deadline feels exciting rather than draining, that is a real signal. If it just sounds stressful, pay attention to that too.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'Can your family\'s finances absorb a low-paid regional-outlet start (Rs 15,000-30,000/month) or an unpaid internship stretch while you build the clips that get you into a stronger outlet? This runway question matters more in journalism than in almost any other writing-adjacent career.',
    note: 'The honest entry period in journalism is often longer and lower-paid than people expect walking in.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'The stable, staff-role segment of Indian journalism is genuinely shrinking as print and TV consolidate. The growing segment is digital and independent, where reach and editorial freedom are real, but income is not guaranteed the way a salary is.',
    note: 'More total reach or influence is not the same as more income security — check which one you actually need right now.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'AI is already doing routine wire-service work — market summaries, weather, sports results, basic press-release rewrites. That layer is genuinely at risk. Original reporting, source relationships, verification judgment, and the willingness to go where the story actually is remain firmly human, and that is where the durable value sits.',
