  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Choosing journalism for the byline, without checking the pay at the specific outlets you are targeting',
    body: 'A visible byline does not always come with strong pay, especially at regional channels where salaries can sit at Rs 15,000-30,000 a month. Check actual entry-level pay at the specific outlets you want to work for, not the outlet\'s brand reputation in general conversation.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Treating "I love writing" as proof you belong in journalism',
    body: 'Loving to write is a real asset, but it is not the same instinct that makes someone want to verify a claim with three sources or knock on a stranger\'s door for a quote. If the writing is the main draw, content writing is worth comparing honestly before you commit years to journalism specifically.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Ignoring the safety conversation because it feels distant',
    body: 'The real risk to field and investigative reporters is documented and concentrated in specific beats — land, mining, communal issues, organised power. It is not evenly spread across every journalism job, but it is real for the reporters who choose those beats, and it deserves a clear-eyed look before you pick a specialisation, not after.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Assuming independent or digital journalism means stable income because it means editorial freedom',
    body: 'Subscriber and sponsor-funded newsrooms and independent creator-journalists can build real reach and real editorial independence, but that income is not a salary. It rises and falls with subscribers, sponsors, and platform reach, and it needs a genuine financial buffer while you build it.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Assuming AI threatens journalism the same way it threatens generic content writing',
    body: 'AI is already handling routine wire-service tasks — market data, weather, results, basic rewrites — but original reporting, verification, and source trust stay human. The mistake is either panicking about AI replacing journalism wholesale, or ignoring it entirely. Learn what it actually automates in this specific field, and build the layer around it.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is journalism a good career in India in 2026?',
    a: 'It depends heavily on which segment you mean. Staff journalism at regional outlets pays poorly (Rs 15,000-30,000/month) and the stable-role pool is shrinking as print revenue declines and outlets consolidate — Indian media layoffs rose roughly 15% year-on-year through 2025. Digital-first and independent journalism has real growth and editorial freedom but unstable, non-salaried income. It is a genuinely good fit for people with real reporting conviction and a financial runway to absorb a slow, uneven start — not a safe default choice for someone who mainly enjoys writing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much do journalists actually earn in India?',
    a: 'Regional print and TV reporters typically start at Rs 15,000-30,000 a month. National print or digital-first outlets pay entry-level reporters roughly Rs 3-8 LPA, usually requiring published clips first. Freelance and stringer income is per-story and highly variable — it can range from a few hundred rupees a piece at a small outlet to a stronger flat fee at a national one. Senior correspondent, bureau chief, or editor pay climbs a lot, but so few of these seats exist that the number matters less than whether the specific outlet is financially healthy enough to still be paying it in three years.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is journalism dangerous in India?',
    a: 'For most desk-based roles, no more than any demanding job. For field and investigative reporters covering land disputes, mining, communal tension, organised crime, or state and corporate power, the risk is real and documented — India consistently ranks in the lower third of global press-freedom indexes, and journalists in these specific beats have faced harassment, legal pressure, and in serious cases, violence. Women journalists also face a documented pattern of coordinated online harassment. The risk concentrates in specific beats; it is not spread evenly across every journalism job.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is independent or YouTube journalism a realistic path in India?',
    a: 'It is a real and growing path — subscriber-funded newsrooms and independent creator-journalists have built genuine reach and editorial independence outside traditional ownership pressure. But the income is not a salary; it depends on subscribers, sponsors, and platform reach, and can swing significantly month to month. Treat it as a real option with a real financial-runway requirement, not as an easier shortcut than traditional journalism.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will AI replace journalists?',
    a: 'AI is already doing routine wire-service work — market summaries, weather updates, sports results, and basic rewrites of official statements. That layer is genuinely shrinking for humans. Original reporting, source verification, investigative judgment, and trust-based relationships with sources stay firmly human, and rising public concern about misinformation is, if anything, raising the value of verified, trusted reporting rather than lowering it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is journalism a good career if I just enjoy writing?',
    a: 'Probably not as the main reason. Journalism asks for verification instinct, comfort with real-time pressure, and tolerance for low starting pay and, in some beats, real safety risk — writing craft is only one part of it. If writing itself is what draws you, content writing is worth comparing honestly first; read our full journalism vs content writing comparison for the fit test on that specific decision.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
