          <div class="mistake-item__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    If your honest answer after all of this is that you are drawn to the writing more than the reporting,
    do not force yourself into journalism out of guilt or prestige — the comparison linked above already
    covers that exact fork.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    A byline you admired on Twitter or one uncle's opinion at a family dinner is not enough evidence to bet
    several years and a stretch of low pay on. Get your own evidence first.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Actually go through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol on paper, with honest, specific answers — not the version
      of yourself that sounds impressive to a relative.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then work through all three Gates on one real, verified story before a degree, an internship stretch,
      or a beat with genuine safety exposure gets locked in.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The financial upside in journalism does not come from the job title on your card. It comes from the
    <strong>reporting and verification skill portfolio</strong> you build underneath it — real clips, a beat
    you genuinely own, and a name sources and editors trust. If you want an outside read on your specific
    situation before you commit, talk to
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career
    guidance</a>, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a> if the
    fit question is still the part you are stuck on.
  </p>

